  "flags": [
    "Legend (p.PDF 9, printed -1-): double-line frame = A/C SORTIE; single thin frame = GROUND TRAINING (CBTS/LESSONS); heavy thick frame = F/S TRAINING; green frame = potential SOLO; orange fill = evaluation; red = night.",
    "EVERY arrow printed in the Contact flow chart is SOLID. The Contact pages contain NO dashed arrow at all, so style is \"solid\" on all 52 diagram edges. Cross-column F/S->A-C arrows are recorded as kind \"feed\" purely from their geometry (horizontal, F/S column into the A/C column), not from a distinct line style.",
    "NO red (night) box appears anywhere in the Contact flow chart: night=false for all 54 boxes.",
    "solo_candidate is taken from the printed green frame (pixel-verified per page: p12 -> C4791; p13 -> C4802,C4803,C4902,C4903,C4904,C5202,C5203; p14 -> C5302,C5303). Ten boxes are green but the mission table (p.PDF 166, printed -158-) allots only 5 SOLO sorties (C47XX,C48XX,C49XX,C52XX,C53XX = 1 each) - consistent with the legend wording \"Potential SOLO flights\": one green box per section is actually flown solo.",
    "C4791 is printed with a GREEN frame (potential/first SOLO), NOT orange. The tasking listed C4791 among the checkrides; the diagram and Contact Special Instruction (3) disagree - the Contact evaluations are exactly C4590, C4790, C5090 and C5490 (C5490 = FINAL CHECKRIDE). checkride=false / first_solo=true recorded for C4791.",
    "C1101 has NO outgoing arrow in print (verified at 4x zoom): B1001 -> B1002 -> C1101 terminates there. C2101 is entered only from ground box IFG 101-102, not from C1101.",
    "The F/S column is NOT a single chain. Printed vertical F/S arrows exist only for B1001->B1002, B1002->C1101, C2101->C2201, C2201->C2202, C2301->C2302, C2402->C2403, C2501->C2502, C2603->C2604. C2503, C2601, C2602 are isolated boxes whose only arrow is the horizontal feed leftwards. No arrow was invented to join them.",
    "Four edges are printed across a page break (arrow leaves the foot of one page and re-enters the head of the next): C2101->C2201 (11->12), AE 101-108->AE 190 (11->12, ground_chain), C4791->C4801 (12->13), C5204->C5301 (13->14). page_pdf on these records the SOURCE page.",
    "\"ground_chain\" is an ADDITIONAL key beyond the requested schema: it holds the 19 printed arrows that run lesson->lesson inside the ground-training column. They are real printed arrows (rule 2) but neither sorties nor ground->sortie entries, so they would otherwise be lost.",
    "Four prereq edges carry one of three ground-lesson ids in \"from\" (\"CR 202\" -> C4101, \"PR 113\" x2 -> B1001 and B1002, \"CO 190\" -> C1101); those ids are ground training units and do NOT appear in \"sorties\". Two of the three are written in the RESTRICTIONS text more narrowly than the flow-chart box that holds the same lesson: \"CR 202\" is inside the printed box \"CR 201-202*\" and \"PR 113\" is inside the printed box \"PR 101-113\" (\"CO 190\" matches its box exactly). Those edges carry a \"printed_box\" field with the box name, so a consumer joining edges against ground_entry_points on node id does not end up with two separate ground nodes for one lesson.",
    "Box-count cross-check passes: 36 A/C-sortie boxes = 31 DUAL + 5 SOLO of the Contact general program (p.PDF 163, printed -155-) and matches the Contact mission table section-by-section (p.PDF 166). 16 Contact F/S boxes (C11XX..C26XX) = \"CONTACT 16 / 20,8\" of the F/S general program (p.PDF 139, printed -131-); B1001/B1002 are the 2 FAMILIARIZATION B10XX F/S sorties.",
    "F/S section heading anomaly in the source: the section covering C2501/C2502/C2503 is printed \"TRAINING SECTION: C2501\" while stating SORTIES/HOURS TOTAL: 3/3,9 (p.PDF 148, printed -140-). Recorded here as section \"C25XX\".",
    "Cross-category dependency NOT recorded as an edge (out of Contact scope): Instrument TRAINING SECTION I41XX, RESTRICTIONS, p.PDF 193 (printed -185-) states \"Prerequisites are IN290, C4791 and I3201.\" - i.e. C4791 gates the start of Instrument flying. To be reconciled by the Instrument digitizer.",
    "The Contact category has exactly EIGHT Special Instructions (p.PDF 165, printed -157-), and this is the complete accounting of all eight. Only (4) generates an edge (C4790 -> C4791, kind \"before\"), and only (3) generates the checkride flags (C4590, C4790, C5090, C5490 = FINAL CHECKRIDE). The remaining six carry NO edge - none of them expresses an order or prerequisite relation between sorties, so no edge is missing: (1) C4101 is not graded in General performance (NG), except for Air Sickness, when SP performance is graded FAIR (59%), no FCJ is flown and training continues at the next block; (2) up to the PRE-SOLO phase max one A/C and one F/S sortie daily, apart from a mission aborted after becoming airborne due to adverse weather or A/C malfunction (MIC); (5) the C4790 evaluator supervises and grades SP's landing from the Contact Car; (6) nose-high unusual attitude recovery is initially taught as a pre-planned maneuver, and only after the SP achieves MIF \"1\" are recoveries practised from different attitudes without previous planning; (7) if the MINIMUM NUMBER of maneuvers is not achieved at the end of the block, it must be annotated in the grade sheet and executed at the next block; (8) ELPs are performed daytime and under VMC conditions only.",
    "Blank pages inside the Contact program analysis (no image, no drawing, no text): p.PDF 174 and p.PDF 180 - nothing was missed there.",
    "The ground-training column is printed on the Contact pages but its boxes are lessons/exams, not sorties; they appear only in ground_entry_points and ground_chain, never in \"sorties\"."
  ]
